content.text:
Now decide on orientation/fullscreen. Let's implement a subtle but effective mobile improvement: attempt landscape lock/fullscreen on start, and if still portrait, show a compact "rotate for best view" hint overlay for the first moments of the match, not blocking. The menu already has a note. Maybe add an in-game portrait hint above the stage: "برای تجربه بهتر، گوشی را افقی بگیرید ↻". It could fade out after a few seconds. This fills the empty space and guides. We can add an HTML element `#rotate-hint` inside #ui, visible only when touch + portrait + game playing, with CSS animation. But main.js needs to show/hide based on screen state and orientation. Simpler: CSS media query shows it whenever portrait and touch device, but it would show on menu too (menu has its own note). We can target `body.touch-device.playing` class, toggled by start/end. There's no `.playing` currently. We can add/remove in startMatch and goMenu/onMatchEnd/pause.

Alternatively, use `body.touch-device:not(.menu-open)` etc. Too much.

Maybe skip the in-game hint; menu note is enough. But adding fullscreen/orientation lock is valuable. Let's implement minimal:

In main.js, `startMatch()` after hideAllScreens:
```
if (isTouch && innerHeight > innerWidth) {
  const el = document.documentElement;
  const request = el.requestFullscreen?.bind(el);
  request?.().then(() => screen.orientation?.lock?.("landscape")).catch(()=>{});
}
```
But fullscreen call is async and requires gesture. If requestFullscreen succeeds, screen.orientation.lock only on Android. On iOS, requestFullscreen undefined. This will make the mobile landscape experience automatic after tapping start in portrait. However, in Playwright with `--mobile`, fullscreen may change viewport? It might not due to headless fullscreen support. Could make testing odd but no crash. Hmm.

Actually if we lock landscape while the physical device is portrait, the viewport rotates to landscape (great). If lock fails but fullscreen succeeds, it remains portrait fullscreen. Fine.

Potential issue: On desktop Firefox, isTouch false. On touch laptops, portrait check prevents. Good.
Potential issue: `requestFullscreen` returns a promise; calling `screen.orientation.lock` after await may break the user-activation requirement (orientation lock requires fullscreen but not user gesture necessarily). It's fine to call inside then.
Let me implement with a helper `enterMobileLandscape()` and catch.

Also `fullscreenchange` / orientationchange triggers resize; already orientationchange listener. Fullscreenchange should also resize; add listener to resize.

Add overlay? Let's also add a small hint in portrait (dismissible? no). Could be CSS-only `#rotate-hint` within #ui, shown when `body.touch-device.portrait-playing`. We need to toggle a class. Let's do this:
- HTML: add `<div id="rotate-hint" class="rotate-hint" hidden>↻ برای تجربه بهتر، گوشی را افقی بگیرید</div>` inside ui.
- CSS: position absolute top: calc(env(safe-area-inset-top) + 10px), left 50%, transform, pill, pointer-events none, z-index. Show only when not hidden.
- main.js: set `gamePlayVisible` class/display when playing. In startMatch, if isTouch && portrait, `rotateHint.hidden = false`; hide on rotate via resize check; hide when paused/menu/result. Add `updateRotateHint()` on resize and orientationchange. Also hide after 6s? Could keep as reminder. It sits above the stage (which is centered with 265px empty top), so not intrusive. Good.
- When the stage is small, this hint fills the top area. Nice.
- Also show the player labels? Not needed.

But on mobile portrait, after entering fullscreen+landscape, the hint hides. If lock fails, hint shows.

Let's add this.

Also, we should make the game area more visible in portrait by filling the background with a subtle decorative radial gradient. Body already. Fine.

Implement HTML insert near end of #ui maybe before touch-controls. Let's add after result-screen. Use `aria-live`? Not necessary.

CSS for `.rotate-hint`.

Main.js logic:
```
const rotateHint = $("#rotate-hint");
function updateRotateHint() {
  const show = isTouch && innerHeight > innerWidth && game.isPlaying && !game.state...? 
}
```
game.isPlaying includes paused and roundEnd/matchEnd; but if result screen shown, game.state matchEnd still true. Need hide when screens result/pause visible. `game.isPlaying` true for matchEnd. Use:
```
const playingScreen = !screens.pause.hidden ? false : screens.result.hidden && game.state !== "menu";
```
Simpler: show if `isTouch && innerHeight > innerWidth && !touchControls.hidden && screens.result.hidden && screens.pause.hidden`. touchControls hidden on result/pause already. In startMatch, setTouchVisible(true) after game.startMatch. But `isTouch` true. Then updateRotateHint.
Call in resize, orientationchange, startMatch, setTouchVisible maybe. Since setTouchVisible is used in pause/result/menu, we can call update after setTouchVisible. But setTouchVisible defined before rotateHint? We'll define rotateHint near top. Modify setTouchVisible:
```
function setTouchVisible(visible) {
  ...
  updateRotateHint();
}
```
But updateRotateHint is function declaration hoisted. Fine.

`updateRotateHint`:
```
function updateRotateHint() {
  const portrait = window.innerHeight > window.innerWidth;
  rotateHint.hidden = !(isTouch && portrait && !touchControls.hidden && screens.result.hidden && screens.pause.hidden && game.state !== "menu");
}
```
At initial load, touchControls hidden, so false.

Potential issue: On mobile portrait while playing, touchControls are visible; hint shows. Good. If in menu, hidden.

Fullscreen/landscape lock function:
```
function requestMobileLandscape() {
  if (!isTouch || window.innerWidth >= window.innerHeight) return;
  const root = document.documentElement;
  if (!root.requestFullscreen || document.fullscreenElement) return;
  root.requestFullscreen({ navigationUI: "hide" }).then(() => {
    return screen.orientation?.lock?.("landscape");
  }).catch(() => {});
}
```
Call in startMatch after hideAllScreens. On iOS requestFullscreen doesn't exist; guarded. Should we avoid auto fullscreen for users who don't want it? For a game, reasonable. Note in final note.

But in a browser, auto fullscreen on button click is accepted. In headless test, might succeed and alter viewport to 360x732 still? Fullscreen doesn't rotate without orientation. Shouldn't crash. We'll test.

Add `document.addEventListener("fullscreenchange", () => setTimeout(resize, 250));`

Also add `screen.orientation?.addEventListener?.("change", ...)` no need orientationchange covers.

Let's implement.

