content.text:
A real bug! `updateProjectiles` line 521 `p.t += dt`, p undefined? How? Probably due to projectile clash handling deleting both projectiles while iterating, then continuing with a stale `p`? Let's look at the code around line 517-591 again. At line 517:
```
for (let i = list.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
  const p = list[i];
  p.t += dt;
  ...
  if (p.x < ... ) { ... list.splice(i,1); continue; }
  // clash
  let clashed=false;
  for (let j=list.length-1; j>=0; j--) {
    ...
    if (clash) {
      ...
      const a = Math.max(i,j);
      list.splice(a,1);
      list.splice(Math.min(i,j),1);
      clashed = true;
      break;
    }
  }
  if (clashed) continue;
  ...
}
```
If i > j? i is current index, j can be greater than i? The inner loop j from list.length-1 down to 0; if a projectile with index j > i clashes, removing index a=Math.max=i? Wait i is current; j can be > i because list could have newer projectiles at higher indices? The outer loop starts from the end and works backwards; i is current highest-ish index; inner j loops from end which may include indices > i? At the start i=list.length-1, so none greater. But after removals, list length shrinks. The outer loop retains i and decrements. Inner j can be > i if i is not the current length-1 due to removals. Removing `a=Math.max(i,j)` may remove an element at j>i, which hasn't been processed by the outer loop yet; then the outer loop later reaches an index that's now beyond the array (since length shrank), causing `list[i]` undefined? Actually the outer loop iterates i downward from the initial `length-1`, but if we remove entries, `list[i]` can be undefined for higher i values that were processed or for lower? Let's trace.

Initial list length 2, i=1 (p2? whichever fired second), j=0 -> clash at p1; a=1, remove index1, then index0. Now list length 0. Outer loop continues; after `clashed` continue, then i-- -> 0; condition i>=0 true; `const p = list[0]` is undefined → `p.t` error. Yes! The bug occurs exactly when a projectile is the current i and the clash removes both, then the outer loop still iterates. `clashed` only continues the current iteration, not break the outer loop; subsequent i values are invalid.

Fix: change outer loop after a clash to `break;` (since both projectiles removed, but there could be more? If a clash removes two, indices below i may still be valid; but simplest break out of updating this frame; all remaining projectiles skip one frame, harmless). Or adjust i: after removing two, decrement i accordingly. Easiest: use `if (clashed) { i--; continue; }`? That would skip the next projectile index which shifted; could process incorrectly. Better: after removing, set `i = Math.min(i, list.length - 1) + 1`? Hmm.

Simplest robust approach: rewrite updateProjectiles to not be index-fragile:
- Iterate backwards; for each p:
  - if p is no longer in list? We can check `if (!list.includes(p)) continue;` but includes is O(n).
  - Use a `removed` flag approach with filtering.
Better: use a `dead` set or mark projectiles for removal. Or handle clash by removing and `break` out of the update loop entirely for this frame (safe, one-frame delay). Since clashes are rare, breaking is fine:
```
let clashed = false;
for (...) {
  ...
  if (clash) { ...; list.splice(a,1); list.splice(Math.min(i,j),1); clashed=true; break; }
}
if (clashed) break;
```
This stops the outer for loop. The remaining projectiles (other than the two) will update next frame; fine.
But note if i and j are the same? excluded.
Also if i or j equal and both removed; break avoids `p.t`.
Let's implement `break;` instead of `continue;` after a clash. Actually if there are more than 2 projectiles, breaking skips updating them one frame; acceptable.

Alternatively fix indices:
- After removing, set `i = Math.min(i, list.length) ...`. But break is simpler and safe.

Also potential similar issue in the `if (p.x < ...)` branch: It removes one and continues; i-- then list shrinks by 1; the next index i-1 is valid? Example length 3, i=2 removed -> length2, i--=1 valid; no problem. If remove 2 via clash, indices above i? None. After removal, i-- -> i-1 might still be >= list.length? If initial length 2, i=1 removed 2 -> length0, i--=0 invalid. So break needed.

Let's patch.

